  "findings": [
    {
      "label": "delivery is AT-LEAST-ONCE. Exactly-once is achieved only WITHIN two windows",
      "detail": "JetStream collapses a repeated Nats-Msg-Id for 120000 ms; the events service collapses a repeated dedupKey inside a 10000 ms bucket. Measured here: 6 deliveries of one result → 1 event inside the window, 2 across it. Consumers must be idempotent."
    },
    {
      "label": "an incident carries the correlation id of the FIRST event in its 60000 ms candidate bucket",
      "detail": "identical candidates collapse on `tenant|rule|group|bucket`, so a burst of matching frames raises one incident — correctly. ⚠️ But the incident then names one frame out of many, and an operator asking \"which frame?\" gets the first, not the one they are looking at. A future Rule Engine milestone that needs every contributing frame must query the events by window, not follow triggeredBy.eventId."
    },
    {
      "label": "trackId and identityId reach the incident by LINK, not by copy",
      "detail": "the incident carries correlationId + triggeredBy.eventId; the subject ids are read back from the event. ⚠️ An event that has aged out of retention while its incident has not breaks that link — the incident remains valid, the subject detail does not."
    },
    {
      "label": "a payload version bump requires NO transport change (ADR-0040)",
      "detail": "payload 1.0.0, 2.0.0 and 3.0.0 — including an unknown scalar and an unknown nested object — all travelled inside envelopeVersion 1.0.0 and were evaluated identically."
    },
    {
      "label": "an envelopeVersion of 2.0.0 is ACCEPTED, not rejected",
      "detail": "correct for an additive minor, and a real gap for a breaking major: a consumer pinned to v1 would process a v2 envelope as though it understood it. No consumer checks the field today. Recorded as a limitation rather than fixed here — the fix is a contract change, and this milestone ships no contract changes beyond ADR-0041."
    }
  ]
